			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1204987">
									

<p>I'm assuming (it's late here, and I'm falling asleep) that you're saying: &quot;Wait, you can't 100% completely render a fractal image because they're infinitely self-similar.&quot; &#8212; and you're right. All of the fractal images we see are actually approximations, and when I said &quot;completed rendering&quot; in the SCP, I meant that the computer finished rendering the image to the level of detail it was programmed to. In-universe, that should be close enough to produce the anomalous effect.</p>
<p>Out-of-character, one of the things I learned while writing this is that for a mathematical SCP to work, it has to make sense to all the non-mathematicians (without them having to go look stuff up, or feeling like they are being taught math instead of reading a story). At the same time, it has to not sound like complete BS to the folks who know more about the underlying math. It's a fine line and I've probably leaned towards staying away from any overly mathematical details.</p>

							</div>

